From 0ea609dc259220dc08b81f6c7e39c6f6983fca36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 18:03:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(device): support the device authorization grant on
 oauthlib 4.0.1

oauthlib 4.0.1 broke the RFC 8628 device flow in two places. Its device
authorization response gained a `scope` key, which RFC 8628 section 3.2
does not define and `DeviceCodeResponse` does not carry, so every
device authorization request failed with a TypeError (HTTP 500). The
device code grant's token request now calls the new validator hook
`validate_device_code(client_id, code, request)`, which `OAuth2Validator`
did not implement, so every poll failed with an AttributeError.

Drop the extra key before building the grant, so the device gets the
RFC response as before, and implement the hook: look the grant up by
device_code and the authenticated client_id, report the RFC 8628
section 3.5 status (expiring the grant past its deadline), and for an
authorized grant set the approving user and the stored scopes on the
request. oauthlib 4.0.0 neither sends the key nor calls the hook, and
keeps working as before.

oauthlib 4.0.1 also validates `scope` at the device authorization
endpoint, so a scope the application may not use is answered with
`invalid_scope` there, as the authorization endpoint already did; the
two device tests that relied on unvalidated scopes are adjusted.

Fixes #1950

